  "step_3": "The decision reason hinges on identifying whether a lottery contains a sure (i.e., 100% certain) outcome and prefers the one without such a sure outcome. However, neither lottery has a sure outcome, so the reason would not lead to a preference. The individual, in contrast, bases their decision on the higher probability of avoiding any loss (i.e., getting 0 Euros), specifically noting that A offers a 75% chance of no loss versus 50% in B. This is a probabilistic comparison, not an evaluation of certainty or sure outcomes. The individual does not mention or consider whether any outcome is guaranteed (100%), which is the core of the decision reason. Therefore, the information used by the individual (probabilities of loss vs no loss) differs from the information required by the decision reason (presence of a 100% outcome). The justification (avoiding loss due to higher probability of zero loss) also differs from the reason's rule (prefer no sure outcome). Since the individual's reasoning is based on likelihoods of outcomes rather than certainty of outcomes, and since the decision reason is inapplicable (as neither has a sure outcome), it is unlikely the individual used this reason. There is no alignment in either the information considered or the decision rule applied.",
